"""appendix_identity -- per-section hash of the appendix against a git revision.

WHY THIS EXISTS. The appendix carries values and frozen blocks that must not drift while the
main text is edited. Reading a whole-file diff cannot tell an authorized appendix addition
from an accidental edit to a committed table. This tool hashes each appendix section
separately, compares against a git revision, and passes only when every changed section is
named in an explicit exemption list.

LINE ENDINGS. Every object is normalized from CRLF to LF before hashing, so a line-ending
difference between the working copy and a git blob can never be reported as a content
difference. This is the convention ADDENDUM_R81.md section 9B binds both Phase 5 instruments
to, and it is stated here because that clause requires each instrument to say so in its own
docstring.

Note that the FROZEN LINEAGE check is a different instrument and a different convention:
paper.tex is hashed as-is, in its CRLF on-disk form, because CLAIMS.md L4 pins that form.
This tool never touches paper.tex.

METHOD
  1. Take the text from \\appendix to \\end{document}.
  2. Split on \\section and \\subsection headings; each heading owns the text up to the next.
  3. Hash each section's LF-normalized bytes.
  4. Compare with the same computation on the given revision.
  5. Report added, removed and changed sections. Exit 0 only when every one of them is
     listed in EXEMPT *and* pinned in PINS to the exact post-edit hash being authorized.

WHY PINS EXIST (R83). Until this revision EXEMPT was keyed by section name alone, so a
section exempted once was exempt for every later change to it, forever. The defect was found
in use rather than in review: a whole-content replacement of the F.1 glossary reported PASS
under an entry authorized for something unrelated ("R81 Phase 3: the glossary row drops
'inherited' under the BRANCH B anonymity ruling"). The gate said PASS for a reason that had
nothing to do with what it was asked to check.

An exemption now authorizes ONE content state, not a section. A named section with no pin, or
with a pin that does not match the current hash, reports UNLISTED. The remaining Pass 3 work
touches most of the appendix, so without this every one of those sections would have acquired
a permanent blanket pass at exactly the moment the gate matters most.

To authorize a change: make the edit, run this tool, copy the hash it prints, and add it to
PINS beside a basis in EXEMPT. Pinning is therefore always a deliberate act with the new
content already in front of you. Once the change is committed the section matches the revision
again and the pin goes quiet; the next edit needs a new one.

Usage:
    python tools/appendix_identity.py [revision] [--list]
        revision defaults to HEAD
        --list prints every section hash and exits 0
"""
